@*
    A sorting question's answer, revealed: the right order, and each player's beside it.

    Two lists rather than one annotated list, because the question this reveal has to answer is
    "where did I go wrong", and that is a comparison. Four short items in two columns makes
    "these two disagree at place three" visible without reading a word; the same information folded
    into one list with markers makes it something to work out.

    Nothing here maps indices to items on its own — see AnswerReveal.SortRows, which is where that
    lookup lives and where it is tested, and which is careful about the one thing that has a
    plausible wrong reading: a stored response is in stored-index terms, not served ones.
*@
@inject Translator L

<div class="sortreveal">
    <div class="sortreveal__col">
        <div class="sortreveal__head">@L["sort.correctOrder"]</div>
        @foreach (var row in AnswerReveal.SortRows(CorrectOrder, CorrectOrder))
        {
            <div class="sortreveal__item sortreveal__item--right" @key="row.Place">
                <span class="sortreveal__place">@L.Num(row.Place + 1)</span>
                <span class="grow" dir="auto">@row.Correct</span>
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    @foreach (var column in Columns)
    {
        <div class="sortreveal__col" @key="column.Label">
            <div class="sortreveal__head" dir="auto">@column.Label</div>
            @foreach (var row in AnswerReveal.SortRows(CorrectOrder, column.Placed))
            {
                <div class="sortreveal__item @ToneOf(row)" @key="row.Place">
                    <span class="sortreveal__place">@L.Num(row.Place + 1)</span>
                    <span class="grow" dir="auto">@(row.Placed ?? "—")</span>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The items in their correct order, as text. Every reveal contract carries exactly this,
    /// whether it calls it <c>CorrectOrder</c> or — on <c>RevealedQuestionDto</c>, where the stored
    /// order <i>is</i> the answer — simply <c>Choices</c>.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public List<string> CorrectOrder { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>One column per player who has an order to show: "You", "Them", or a name.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public List<SortRevealColumn> Columns { get; set; } = [];

    private static string ToneOf(SortRevealRow row) => row.Placed is null
        ? "sortreveal__item--blank"
        : row.Right ? "sortreveal__item--right" : "sortreveal__item--wrong";
}
